WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.982
Medical affairs teams answer literature questions from doctors in the field.

2
00:00:04.032 --> 00:00:07.999
Each one means finding the right papers and reading them by hand.

3
00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:12.016
I built a tool that does that search and returns an answer

4
00:00:12.066 --> 00:00:13.020
with its sources.

5
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:14.838
This is MedRAG.

6
00:00:14.888 --> 00:00:18.991
It searches 498 recent PubMed papers on GLP-1 drugs and heart outcomes,

7
00:00:19.041 --> 00:00:21.067
278 of them in full text.

8
00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:27.642
Ask the live demo a clinical question and within seconds it gives an answer that quotes the hazard ratio,

9
00:00:27.692 --> 00:00:32.140
with every claim linked to the paper and the passage it came from.

10
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.474
Under the hood, keyword search and semantic search run side by side,

11
00:00:37.524 --> 00:00:40.829
their results are fused, and a cross-encoder reranks them.

12
00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:44.930
A LangGraph agent plans the search, splits questions with several parts,

13
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.540
and writes a typed answer with citations.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.559
I didn't assume the agent helped.

15
00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.829
I scored ten configurations with confidence intervals, and the first agent made retrieval worse:

16
00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:57.335
recall dropped by 0.20.

17
00:00:57.385 --> 00:01:00.723
Every step is traced, and the traces showed why.

18
00:01:00.773 --> 00:01:04.111
Its sub-queries found the right papers, then lost them,

19
00:01:04.161 --> 00:01:07.500
because it merged reranker scores computed against different queries.

20
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.770
Re-scoring the merged pool fixed it, and the fixed agent matched the plain pipeline.

21
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.964
Most questions turned out to have one part, so those now skip the loop:

22
00:01:19.014 --> 00:01:22.795
same recall, median latency down from 14 seconds to 6.5.

23
00:01:22.845 --> 00:01:25.860
On questions with several parts, the loop wins.

24
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.027
Indexing full text instead of abstracts more than tripled the answers that

25
00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:32.503
quote a confidence interval.

26
00:01:32.553 --> 00:01:36.562
I checked the result on 20 questions never used for tuning.

27
00:01:36.612 --> 00:01:40.620
It's tested, type-checked in CI, and live on a public demo.

28
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.210
I built cited answers in six and a half seconds from 498 papers,

29
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.650
matching the strongest baseline, by pairing hybrid retrieval with an evaluation harness

30
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.240
whose traces showed where the agent helps.
